{
  // X-GIS #1681 B1 — the PACKAGE-LOCAL base, never the monorepo root's: this directory
  // must compile when it is vendored out of the repo. See tsconfig.base.json.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "outDir": "./dist",
    // The package root, NOT ./src, so the emit mirrors the source tree:
    // src/index.ts -> dist/src/index.js and examples/index.ts -> dist/examples/index.js.
    // This is forced, not cosmetic. `tsc` does not rewrite import specifiers, so the
    // `from '../src/index.js'` that every examples file carries is copied verbatim into
    // dist/examples/*.js — and it only resolves if the src output really is one directory
    // up under `src/`. Emitting src to dist/ root (the old layout) pointed every emitted
    // example at dist/src/, which did not exist, and `typeshade/examples` failed at the
    // first hop. Nothing else in the package names a dist path: the exports map published
    // to npm is DERIVED from this layout by scripts/publish-manifest.ts.
    "rootDir": ".",
    // `.d.ts.map` beside every `.d.ts`. The tarball ships src/ and examples/ next to
    // dist/ (`files` in package.json keeps both), so a consumer's "go to definition" lands
    // on the real TypeScript rather than on a generated declaration.
    "declarationMap": true,
    "types": [],
    // X-GIS #1686 — nodenext is the RATCHET, not a preference. `tsc` does not rewrite import
    // specifiers, so an extensionless `from './core/ir'` is copied verbatim into
    // `dist/*.js` and `dist/*.d.ts`, and Node ESM resolves neither an extensionless
    // path nor a directory: every entry point failed at the first hop with
    // ERR_UNSUPPORTED_DIR_IMPORT. Every relative specifier in this package now carries
    // an explicit `.js`, and under nodenext a missing one is TS2835 — a build error, so
    // the class of bug cannot come back silently.
    //
    // WHY HERE AND NOT IN tsconfig.base.json: that file is a pinned COPY of the repo
    // root's baseline and `src/tsconfig-drift.test.ts` fails on any value that differs
    // from the root's. The root is `module: ESNext` / `moduleResolution: bundler` for
    // the bundled packages; only shader-dsl is published as a standalone artifact, so
    // the override belongs in this package's leaf configs (this file,
    // tsconfig.tests.json, examples/tsconfig.json) — all three, or the editor project
    // and the emitting project would disagree about what resolves.
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext"
  },
  // examples/ joins the EMITTING project because `./examples` is a public `exports`
  // subpath and a published subpath has to resolve to built .js/.d.ts like every other one.
  // The noEmit pass (tsconfig.tests.json) still type-checks all of examples/ and scripts/;
  // this list is about what reaches dist/.
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "examples/**/*.ts"],
  // X-GIS #763 V6 — tests never reach dist/ (compiled *.test.js/.d.ts were riding along
  // into the build output). Test TYPE-CHECKING is not lost: the
  // `build` script chains `tsc -p tsconfig.tests.json` (a noEmit pass over src + examples).
  //
  // `examples/**/*.shade.ts` is "use typeshade" SOURCE, not TypeScript — the same exclusion
  // tsconfig.tests.json and examples/tsconfig.json carry, for the same reason.
  //
  // The ten named examples files are everything in that directory NOT reachable from
  // `examples/index.ts`, which is what the `./examples` subpath resolves to: repository-local
  // gates (`_goldens`, `_order`, `_scan`, `_twin-diff`, `_mrt-gate`, `_shade`), CLIs
  // (`print`, `measure`) and two host-integration snippets nothing imports. The list is not
  // hand-curated — `src/publish-manifest.test.ts` D4 recomputes the closure with the
  // TypeScript API and fails if this list and "unreachable from index.ts" disagree, so an
  // example added to the registry is emitted automatically and a helper added beside it is
  // reported. Three reasons to draw the line there rather than emit the whole directory:
  //   1. `_shade`, `_scan` and `_goldens` read the filesystem RELATIVE TO THEIR OWN FILE
  //      (`dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`), so their dist copies would look for
  //      `.shade.ts` sources and `__emit-goldens__/` inside dist/examples/ and find neither.
  //      Emitting them ships modules that cannot work.
  //   2. `host-webgpu.ts` needs the `@webgpu/types` GLOBALS, and `types: []` above withholds
  //      them on purpose — it is what proves the published .d.ts carry no ambient dependency
  //      a consumer has to install. (`host-webgl2.ts` compiles fine; WebGL2 is in the DOM
  //      lib. It is excluded as unreachable, not as a type failure.)
  //   3. `exports` has no wildcard subpath, so none of the ten is importable from the
  //      published package anyway. Emitting them adds only weight.
  // Every one of them is still TYPE-CHECKED, by `tsc -p tsconfig.tests.json`, which covers
  // all of examples/ and scripts/ and does load those types.
  //
  // The two `coredef-*` modules beside `coredef-overloads.test.ts` are its support: the claims
  // on Tint's table and the both-halves witness (0017). No export reaches them, so they are
  // test code in all but name, and they stay out of dist/ as the tests do.
  "exclude": [
    "src/**/*.test.ts",
    "src/core/spec-conformance/coredef-overlay.ts",
    "src/core/spec-conformance/coredef-witness.ts",
    "examples/**/*.test.ts",
    "examples/**/*.shade.ts",
    "examples/_goldens.ts",
    "examples/_mrt-gate.ts",
    "examples/_order.ts",
    "examples/_scan.ts",
    "examples/_shade.ts",
    "examples/_twin-diff.ts",
    "examples/host-webgl2.ts",
    "examples/host-webgpu.ts",
    "examples/measure.ts",
    "examples/print.ts"
  ]
}
